Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Slab chunks and cache objects as Sublet lifetimes (CDERIVE/CREVOKE/CRENEW)
Upstream: memcached 1.6.45
Upstream-Archive: https://memcached.org/files/memcached-1.6.45.tar.gz
Upstream-SHA256: d362c64e6d8d5287153501eabf7c85b4a761432fbf53f5d7b085d0bb1653c1dd
Build-Variants: only with -DMC_CAPSTONE_SUBLET, on the virtual profile (musl mallocng, object
  lifetimes on), with -I capstone/runtime/include (capability.h); every other build is unchanged
Depends-On: memcached-1.6.45-0001-capability-alignment.patch
Apply-From: upstream source root, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1

memcached's slab allocator never frees a chunk: do_slabs_free files it on its class's free list
and do_slabs_alloc hands it out again, and cache.c keeps freed objects on a list the same way. A
stale item or buffer pointer therefore reaches whatever the chunk holds next. Three instructions,
inserted where the allocators hand memory out and take it back, end that
(docs/design/virtual-capstone/isa.md): CDERIVE makes a child capability of a non-linear parent,
bounded to a sub-range; CREVOKE(object) ends the object its one operand names and everything
derived inside it, and faults when that object is not live; CRENEW(object) ends it the same way
and begins a new lifetime in the same place. Neither names a parent or needs a right.

Layout. The patch adds no field and changes no size: item and item_chunk, the chunk sizes, the
chunks per page, the pages and every malloc request stay upstream's. The allocator keeps its
authority where upstream keeps its pages: each class's slab_list holds every page as
memory_allocate returned it, and that reference covers the page. The allocators leave none of
their own references, to a page or to another free chunk or object, in memory they hand out.
What a chunk's previous owner wrote into it stays there, as upstream leaves it.

Slabs. Each page is one lifetime, the one its slab_list reference names:
- do_slabs_alloc returns a CDERIVE child of the page, bounded to the class's chunk size. It
  derives the child from the free-list entry, which is the allocator's own pointer into the page,
  and clears the entry's link before the chunk leaves the list.
- slabs_free ends the caller's chunk (CREVOKE). It then finds the page that holds the chunk's
  address by address range over the class's slab_list, and over every class's if that fails,
  because a chunked item's header comes from a class other than the one its owner passes.
  do_slabs_free continues through that page reference, moved to the chunk, and writes its
  bookkeeping into the released chunk as upstream does. do_slabs_free_chunked takes back each
  chained chunk of a large item the same way. Nothing is read through the caller's reference.
- slabs_finalize_page_move renews the page's lifetime (CRENEW) when the page leaves its class.
  That ends, in one instruction, every chunk the page handed out, including the ones the mover
  rescued or reclaimed in place without a free. The page goes on under the renewed reference, in
  its new class or in the global page pool.
- With -L, memory_allocate cuts every page from one preallocated block. It derives each page as a
  child of the block (CDERIVE), so that renewing one page ends no other page's chunks.

Bounds. The compact bounds format holds a range of 4 KiB or more only on its granule,
2^(msb(len)-9) (capstone/representable.h), and CDERIVE refuses any other range (cause 29). The
chunks of the classes from 16 KiB up rarely start and end on a granule, so sublet_derive derives
the smallest representable range around each: a large chunk reaches less than one granule, at
most 1/512 of its length, into its neighbours. The cursor stays at the chunk. Placement is
upstream's.

do_slabs_alloc bounds a chunk to the chunk size and not to the request: slabs_alloc does not
receive the request, and the mover copies whole chunks (memcpy of cls_size into a chunk it
allocated).

The mover walks a page through the page's own reference. A CREVOKE through that reference would
end the page and every chunk in it at once. For a linked item (MOVE_FROM_LRU, MOVE_BUSY_ACTIVE)
the mover therefore takes the hash table's reference to it under the item lock, and every unlink,
replace or remove that may free it uses that reference.

Cache. Every cache object is one malloc, so it already has a lifetime: the one the system
allocator gave it. cache_alloc hands out the pointer malloc returned, as upstream does, and clears
the free-list link in the object, which named the next free object. cache_free renews the
object's lifetime (CRENEW), which ends every copy of the caller's pointer, and files the renewed
pointer on the free list. cache_destroy passes free() that pointer, which names the allocation's
current lifetime. The layout of an object is upstream's, with or without the debug redzone. This
relies on the system allocator giving each object a lifetime of its own: on a heap without object
lifetimes the pointer would name something larger, and renewing it would end that.

Running out of lifetimes. When the lifetime table has no slot left and cannot grow, CDERIVE and
CRENEW return an untagged 0 and change nothing. do_slabs_alloc derives the chunk before it leaves
the free list and returns NULL, memcached's own out-of-memory path. memory_allocate then gives no
page from the preallocated block. slabs_finalize_page_move ends a page it cannot renew (CREVOKE)
and drops it: memcached loses the page's memory, and no chunk the page handed out stays live.
do_cache_free frees an object whose lifetime it cannot renew, as it does over the cache's limit.

Costs and limits. slabs_free searches its class's page list, so its cost grows with the pages of
the class. Restart mode (-e) rebuilds pages and items from addresses in the restored block, so
their references name the whole block: the first free or page move ends the block, and the next
access faults.

